==============================================================
2. Labels -- how many and against which clusters
==============================================================

Each makes one Label_0, Label_1, ... column (a different random
seed each), so you can compare several labellings of the same data.
  How many synthetic labels to generate [1]: 
Most datasets have 'labels0'. Leave as-is unless you know there are more (e.g. 'labels1').
  Which ground-truth labeling to match [labels0]:   Random seed for label generation [42]: 
==============================================================
3. Cluster-label matching -- the core settings
==============================================================

How many DIFFERENT label values to create. This is NOT the total number
of labels -- every datapoint always gets a label -- it's how many classes
they split into (e.g. 3 -> values 0, 1, 2), sized to your whole dataset.
  Number of labels (M) [3]: 
How should the new label relate to your clusters?
  perfect : an exact copy, one label per cluster (score MCC = 1.0).
            Needs M = your cluster count.
  single  : ONE cluster becomes one label; all other points are unrelated.
  random  : the label ignores the clusters completely (MCC ~ 0) -- a baseline.
  custom  : you write rules (send a share of a label into chosen clusters).
            Use for partial/realistic agreement, or to aim at a target score.
    1) perfect
    2) single
    3) random
    4) custom
  Matching mode [custom]: 
balanced   : every label is roughly the same size.
unbalanced : some labels are bigger than others (like real data,
             where one class is often much more common).
    1) balanced
    2) unbalanced
  Label balance [balanced]: 
Normally YOU set how much the label matches (recall, below).
Say yes to instead name a target MCC/ARI and let the tool solve
for it. Caveat: a target can be impossible for your data (e.g.
MCC=1 with fewer labels than clusters) -- then it gets as close
as it can and tells you it fell short.
  Aim for a specific agreement score instead of setting it by hand? (yes/no) [no]:   Which cluster id becomes the aligned label? (an id from your cluster column, e.g. 1):   Which label value (0..M-1) it becomes [0]: 
Optional 'structured noise': points NOT claimed by your rules normally get
filler labels from the spillover rule. This instead forces a share of ONE
cluster's leftover points to a single competing label, placed at that
cluster's edge (or center). Consequences to be aware of:
  - it BYPASSES your proportions: final label sizes will no longer match
    them (same caveat as the uniform/concentrated spillover rules);
  - it CHANGES the agreement score: structured noise scores differently
    from random noise -- comparing the two is exactly what it is for;
  - it only shapes points not already claimed by the rules above.
  Give one cluster's leftover points a specific competing label (structured noise)? (yes/no) [no]: 
  Competing-noise entry 1:
    which cluster id gets the competing label:       which label value (0..M-1) competes there [0]:       share of that cluster's LEFTOVER points to convert (0..1) [1.0]: 
boundary : the cluster's rim (the usual 'hard case').
core     : the cluster's center.
random   : anywhere in the cluster.
    1) boundary
    2) core
    3) random
      where the competing label sits in the cluster [boundary]:     Add another competing-noise entry? (yes/no) [no]: 
  Competing-noise entry 2:
    which cluster id gets the competing label:       which label value (0..M-1) competes there [0]:       share of that cluster's LEFTOVER points to convert (0..1) [1.0]: 
boundary : the cluster's rim (the usual 'hard case').
core     : the cluster's center.
random   : anywhere in the cluster.
    1) boundary
    2) core
    3) random
      where the competing label sits in the cluster [boundary]:     Add another competing-noise entry? (yes/no) [no]: 
Optional and purely spatial: choose whether the labelled points sit near the
cluster CENTER (core) or its EDGE (boundary). This does NOT change the score
-- only which points carry the label -- handy for testing how a metric reacts
to geometry.
  Control WHERE inside each cluster the labelled points sit (center vs edge)? (yes/no) [no]: 
